oliver_cs: FYI onboarding customer 'Cedar' reporting a burst of 429s + intermittent `insufficient_quota` during Node SDK warmup (~210 parallel clients). Can infra advise quick mitigations?

mina_eng: Hi @oliver_cs 10: likely a preflight fanout. Quick Qs: are they calling preflight on every session/tab and creating ephemeral tokens per request?

oliver_cs: Yes the quickstart was followed: `await client.preflight()` on every new user session. Logs: 
```
[2026-08-02T11:02:10Z] uid=ced-01 auth=ok token_age=2s status=200
[2026-08-02T11:02:10Z] uid=ced-02 auth=failed error=insufficient_quota
[2026-08-02T11:02:10Z] uid=ced-03 status=429 message="rate limit exceeded"
```

mina_eng: Short, actionable mitigations: (1) stop preflight-per-tab, (2) cache preflight in-process for 30-60s, (3) splay the warmup calls with small random delays, (4) use one ephemeral token per session instead of per-request, (5) enable http2 keepalive/connection reuse. Minimal code to share: 
```
// process-level cached preflight + splay
let _pf = {ts:0,res:null};
async function cachedPreflight(client){
  if (Date.now()-_pf.ts < 40000) return _pf.res; // 40s cache
  await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, Math.floor(Math.random()*250)+50));
  _pf = {ts: Date.now(), res: await client.preflight()};
  return _pf.res;
}
```

ratebot: Alert: key=rw_live_**** triggered onboarding-throttle at 2026-08-02T11:02:10Z. Window=120s, peak_rps=60, avg_rps=14. Recommend lowering concurrent preflight <=20 or request temporary warmup bump.

marco_support: Good callout. CS can file a short support ticket for a 10-minute warmup increase if they need >20 concurrency for a one-off ramp. Otherwise these client changes usually collapse the stampede and reduce 429s to <5%. If they share canary timestamps we'll correlate logs and lift throttles briefly.

oliver_cs: Thanks 10: applying 40s cache + splay + reduce parallelism to 18, will run canary and post results + ticket id if needed.
